January ٢٦, ٢٠٢٦

To: UN Women

Subject: Urgent Report: Systematic Crimes, Violence, and Desecration of the Bodies of Female Fighters

To Whom It May Concern,

I am writing to you with a heavy heart to bring to your immediate attention a series of grave violations and inhumane acts being perpetrated against women, specifically targeting female fighters. These actions are not only war crimes but also a profound assault on human dignity and women’s rights globally.
We have documented several horrifying incidents that demand urgent international investigation and unequivocal condemnation:
١. Desecration of the Deceased: The act of throwing the lifeless body of a female fighter from a height is a barbaric violation of international humanitarian law and the sanctity of the dead. This is a clear attempt to dehumanize women who fight for their freedom.
٢. Gender-Based Humiliation: The intentional cutting of a female fighter’s hair as a 'trophy' or for the purpose of degradation is a direct attack on her identity and gender. Such acts are used as tools of psychological warfare to undermine the symbol of female strength.
٣. Systematic Violence: These are not isolated incidents; they are part of a broader pattern of systematic violations and misogyny aimed at silencing women who stand against oppression.
The silence of the international community in the face of these atrocities only serves to embolden the perpetrators. We urgently call upon your organization to:
• Officially condemn these acts of violence and desecration.
• Launch an independent investigation into these crimes committed against female fighters.
• Take all legal measures to identify the perpetrators, bring them before international courts, and ensure they face just punishment.
• Exert pressure on governments and relevant parties to end the culture of impunity.
Women’s rights are universal, and the dignity of a woman—whether in life or in death—must be protected at all costs. We look forward to your immediate response and decisive action regarding this urgent matter.

Sincerely,

Said Saddik